      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the \\(x\\)-intercept, we set \\(y = 0\\) in the equation of the parabola:\n\n\\[ x = 2(0)^2 - 6(0) + 3 = 3. \\]\n\nThus, the \\(x\\)-intercept is \\((3, 0)\\). So, \\(a = 3\\).\n\nNext, we find the \\(y\\)-intercepts. The \\(y\\)-intercepts occur when \\(x = 0\\). Setting \\(x = 0\\) in the equation of the parabola, we get:\n\n\\[ 0 = 2y^2 - 6y + 3. \\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(y = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 3\\):\n\n\\[ y = \\frac{-(-6) \\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^2 - 4 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3}}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 24}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{12}}{4} = \\frac{6 \\pm 2\\sqrt{3}}{4} = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}. \\]\n\nThus, the \\(y\\)-intercepts are \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\) and \\(\\left(0, \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)\\). So, \\(b = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(c = \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\nTo find \\(b + c\\), we add these two values together:\n\n\\[ b + c = \\frac{3 + \\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{3 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{(3 + \\sqrt{3}) + (3 - \\sqrt{3})}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3. \\]\n\nTherefore, \\(a + b + c = 3 + 3 = 6\\).\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
